From 5f72c76978b395c05424be1b1cdbac350328363f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: INTEL-CAMERA WIP <wip@localhost>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 21:36:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] BOZZA media: i2c: Add GC8034 image sensor driver

NON INVIABILE: attribuzione Rockchip non sciolta, manca il
Signed-off-by.

PROVATO SU HARDWARE il 2026-08-11 su CHUWI Hi10 X1: chip ID 0x8044
letto, cattura a 3264x2448, guadagno analogico 7,66x chiesti e 7,9x
misurati, v4l2-compliance 45/46.

Driver for the GalaxyCore GC8034, structured after gc08a3.c with the
same x86/ACPI adaptations as gc5035.c. Register semantics were derived
from the Rockchip BSP driver: 8-bit paged registers, analogue gain as
an index into a lookup table with digital gain making up the remainder,
and a blanking register holding an offset rather than the frame length
(VTS = reg + 2484).

Those tables were computed for a 24 MHz external clock, while an IPU6
platform provides 19.2 MHz. They work unmodified at that rate: every
timing simply scales with the clock, which is what the fixed PLL setup
in the tables implies. Both the link frequency and the pixel array rate
are therefore derived from the clock instead of being hardcoded.

Measured on hardware, the frame rate is 23.99 fps where the tabulated
30.00 belongs to 24 MHz: a ratio of 0.7997 against the 0.8000 of
19.2/24. The link frequency here is thus 268.8 MHz and not the 336 MHz
the BSP declares, both being the same multiplier of 14. This also
settles the discrepancy in the BSP, whose table comment claims 656 Mbps
per lane, that is 328 MHz: that figure is simply wrong.

For the same reason the delay before the first I2C access is expressed
in xvclk cycles: the BSP hardcoded 350 us, which is its 8192 cycles at
24 MHz and too short at 19.2 MHz, where they last 427 us.

Target hardware: CHUWI Hi10 X1 (Intel N100, Alder Lake-N, IPU6),
ACPI HID GCTI8034 at \_SB.PC00.LNK0.
